architecture closure-algebra
  canonical_name ClosureAlgebra
  family capability
  domain Capability
  maturity measured
  semantic_question What closes, what blocks, and what would one more commitment unlock -- for any domain that states its own conditions?
  # Four things in this estate turned out to be one shape. Reconciliation asks
  # whether obligations settle, funding whether capital routes close, feasibility
  # whether a desired future can exist, and a birth operation whether an object
  # may enter the world. Each is a desired state, conditions in several states of
  # satisfaction, resolvers that close conditions and carry their own, and a
  # closure repeated until nothing new closes.
  #
  #     Closure(S) = mu X . ( S union { effects(r) : r in admissible(X) } )
  #
  # The algebra is generic and the grammar is not: which conditions exist and
  # what a resolver closes are domain facts that stay in the declaration.
  node_types Condition
  node_types Resolver
  node_types ClosureDomain
  edge_types requires
  edge_types satisfies
  state_types SATISFIED
  state_types UNKNOWN
  state_types BLOCKED
  invariant an unknown condition is not an unmet one; collapsing the two reports a wall where there is only silence, and nobody asked is a different answer from refused
  invariant a resolver below the admitting rung is real and not yet load-bearing; a list of expressions of interest must not present itself as a feasible world, and the ladder has eleven rungs rather than two
  invariant a resolver held below the rung is not also reported as blocked; not yet committed and blocked on a condition have different fixes
  invariant a resolver is worth the set of conditions that stop closing without it, measured by removing it and recomputing; pricing it against the one condition it closes directly understates it by the rest of its closure
  invariant a declared cost of zero means free and an absent cost means unpriced; treating them alike buried the best trades among the unknowns, and an unpriced resolver never carries a computed value
  invariant every counterfactual is the whole closure run again with the condition treated as met, never an estimate of what it might unlock
  invariant the fixed point is bounded by the resolver count, so a cycle in the declaration ends the loop rather than spinning it
  forbidden_inference a closed condition -> a condition satisfied in the world
  forbidden_inference a fixed point -> a plan
  forbidden_inference an interested resolver -> a committed one
  forbidden_inference a reachable world -> a chosen one
  computational_form derivation
  native_format json
  source_path scripts/closure-algebra.mjs
  source_path scripts/closure_algebra.py.archive
  witness scripts/tests/closure-algebra.test.mjs exercises work-list fixed-point closure, UNKNOWN separation, commitment thresholds, marginal leverage, reuse, declared cost and probability faults; one-time Python comparison matched the complete receipt except run timestamp
  witness cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/tests/closure_algebra_test.sh
  measured two domains through one evaluator: capital over institution:nightgrid reproduces the funding result including that the capital condition unlocks nothing alone, and feasibility over a declared world shows venue-agreement closing one condition directly while five fail without it
  measured enforcing the commitment ladder moved the declared world from seven of eight conditions closing to zero, because every resolver sat at unknown; committing one moved it to two, which is the cascade the ladder exists to gate
  known_gap the requirement key is found by convention from the declared type name, so a domain naming its key differently would need the convention stated in the declaration rather than inferred
  known_gap a source this entry named could not be opened from this checkout -- /var/lib/bonfyre/cms/content-types/closure_domain.json. The line is removed rather than repointed to something that merely looks similar, and what remains listed is the evidence this architecture actually stands on.
architecture closure-loop
  canonical_name ClosureLoop
  family capability
  domain Capability
  maturity measured
  semantic_question From an unmet condition to a scored outcome, what does each stage claim and what does it refuse to claim?
  # A residual is a precise statement of something this institution needs and
  # cannot obtain, which is the input a live gym wants. It becomes a target with
  # several phrasings, a spend decision justified by what it would close, an
  # effect performed by whoever owns it, and a prediction scored against what
  # happened. Each stage refuses the next stage's ground.
  node_types ResidualTarget
  node_types SpendDecision
  node_types Prediction
  node_types Outcome
  edge_types names
  edge_types justifies
  edge_types scores
  state_types named
  state_types admitted
  state_types scored
  invariant a residual target is typed by the condition's own declared kind and never by the class of whatever is blocked on it; taking the blocker's class typed a partner requirement as evidence because a sponsor was waiting on it
  invariant a phrasing must not repeat the condition's own word back at it; a template that produced permit permit is the same question asked twice rather than a second way of asking
  invariant naming a target reaches the first rung of six and says so; a named target is not a declared capability, an admissible route, an executed trial, a retrieved result or a closed requirement
  invariant a spend decides admissibility and holds no credential; a decision to spend is not a payment, and the two do not live in the same place
  invariant an unpriced resolver is never admitted, because a purchase with no ceiling has no ceiling; and nothing is bought that closes nothing, however cheap
  invariant a spend above the policy ceiling is referred to a person rather than trimmed to fit, because a ceiling that bends is a suggestion
  invariant a prediction is recorded before the change and scored after; an unchanged closure is not evidence the prediction was right
  forbidden_inference a named target -> a measured market
  forbidden_inference an admitted spend -> an approved one, or a payment
  forbidden_inference a correct prediction -> a good decision
  forbidden_inference one cohort agreeing -> a validated planner
  computational_form derivation
  native_format json
  source_path scripts/residual-target.mjs
  source_path scripts/residual-vocabularies.cue
  source_path scripts/residual_target.py.archive
  witness scripts/tests/residual-target.test.mjs proves authored vocabularies preserve typed residual targets and do not duplicate a condition's own vocabulary in phrasings
  source_path scripts/closure-spend.mjs
  source_path scripts/closure-outcome.mjs
  source_path scripts/closure_spend.py.archive
  source_path scripts/closure_outcome.py.archive
  witness scripts/tests/closure-spend.test.mjs proves a pure spending decision refuses unpriced, free, inert and over-ceiling resolvers without moving money
  witness scripts/tests/closure-outcome.test.mjs proves prediction receipts reject cross-subject scoring and an unchanged closure never confirms a prediction
  witness cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/tests/closure_loop_test.sh
  measured a prediction recorded over a declared world, one resolver then committed, and the score found two conditions closed of which one had been predicted to unlock nothing -- the planner wrong in a way it can now see
  measured a $300 capture is referred at a $10 information ceiling and admitted at $500 once the world is valued at $30,000; no money moves at either
  known_gap no external route is credentialed for any named capability, so every target stops at the first rung and the market behind it is unmeasured rather than absent

architecture capability-discovery-gap
  canonical_name CapabilityDiscoveryGap
  family capability
  domain Capability / Registry
  layer capability
  semantic_question Can this estate discover a capability it has just gained?
  purpose BonfyreCapability indexes 103 capabilities with real cost estimates and latency tiers and is what capability matching reads. It cannot find a capability that was added after it was compiled, which is why work done in the checkout is invisible to the estate's own discovery until someone edits C.
  maturity measured
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCapability/src/main.c
  publishes CandidateClassification
  internal_calculus the capability set is a compiled-in BUILTINS table plus rows seeded into the index database once. `index` calls rebuild_index, which rebuilds an FTS index over the rows that already exist, and sync_catalog, which syncs recipes from the repository. Neither enumerates binaries
  invariant a command named index rebuilds an index and does not discover; reading it as discovery is what made twenty-five newly installed organs look like a no-op rather than like an unsupported operation
  cannot_infer an installed organ -> a discoverable capability; nineteen organs built in the checkout were installed into the managed binary directory and the registry still reported the same 103
  cannot_infer a capability absent from the registry -> a capability the estate cannot execute; native_tool_invoke reaches every installed binary and capability_fulfillment routes any named capability across eight carriers. What is missing is discovery, not execution
  witness installing nineteen checkout-only organs raised the managed binary count from 98 to 123 and left `bonfyre-capability list` at 103, with none of the newly installed organs appearing
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCapability/tests/discovery_test.sh
  internal_calculus index now scans the directory the binary itself lives in, which is the directory the estate installs into, and records anything named bonfyre-* that the builtin table does not already claim. A curated row is never overwritten by a scan, because it carries a measured cost and a latency tier and a scan knows neither
  invariant a discovered organ is recorded at cost -1, meaning unmeasured, and never 0. Zero is a price, and this estate already refuses elsewhere to let an unpriced capability read as a free one; both list renderers print it as unmeasured rather than as a number
  invariant discovery is idempotent; a second index reports nothing newly discovered rather than re-counting what it already holds
  witness cmd/BonfyreCapability/tests/discovery_test.sh runs on every build against a synthetic install tree holding an organ no table could know about: it must be discovered, it must carry no price, running index twice must discover nothing further, and a curated cost must survive the scan
  witness discovery over the real install tree took the registry from 103 to 134, finding all 31 organs that had been installed and were invisible, including every organ this session worked in
  source_path architecture/atlas.mjs
  internal_calculus a discovered organ is described by the atlas rather than by the scan. Every organ already carried a semantic_question, a layer, a family, a domain and a maturity in the entry whose source_path points into its directory; the registry reads a generated projection of that, architecture/atlas.organs.json, rather than parsing the 258KB index. An organ with no entry is recorded as undescribed and is never given a description this tool invented
  internal_calculus match scores the registry and not the compiled-in table. It scored BUILTINS while list read the database, so a discovered capability was listable and unmatchable -- the registry could show an organ it had just found and then fail to return it for the need it answers, which made discovery cosmetic. A curated row still competes on its curated keywords and a discovered row competes on what the atlas says about it
  invariant there is one scorer. The compiled-table scorer was removed rather than left beside the registry one, because two scorers that disagree is the same failure this entry exists to record
  cannot_infer a description from the atlas -> a measured cost; the atlas knows what an organ is for and not what it costs to run, so a discovered row stays unmeasured on both cost and latency
  witness the extended discovery witness asserts a discovered organ is matchable and not merely listable, and that a curated capability still matches on its own keywords
  witness bonfyre-lex scores 0.714 against its own semantic_question, above bonfyre-spatial at 0.429, where before neither could be returned at all
  internal_calculus a capability is matched on its id as well as its description, keywords and name. The id is the shortest true name a capability has -- sign, lex, spatial -- and was the one field never scored, so a caller asking for a thing by its own name got nothing back
  internal_calculus camel case is split before tokenising. BonfyreSign is one token to a splitter, so the organ whose entire purpose is signing did not rank for the verb sign
  invariant a capability ranks for its own name; the witness installs an organ with an invented name and requires it to be returned both by its id and by a word inside its camel-case name
  witness bonfyre-sign now ranks first for "sign a statement so a third party can verify it", above bonfyre-proof; bonfyre-spatial scores 1.200 for its own subject and bonfyre-lex 1.000
  internal_calculus a learned metric is available and is not the default, because that is what the measurement said. Over ten queries with known answers, word overlap ranked the right capability first nine times and fusing a learned cosine managed seven
  internal_calculus the embedding organ refuses to return a hashed-token vector when a learned one was requested, on the grounds that hashed-token geometry is not a learned metric and distances are not comparable across the two. This registry honours the same refusal: it never falls back to the hash backend, it falls back to word overlap and says so
  invariant a ranking names the metric that produced it. A caller shown a number alone cannot tell a learned distance from a word count, and the two do not mean the same thing
  invariant two metrics are fused by rank and never by score. Scaling cosine to compete with word overlap made every row land within 0.02 of every other and turned the lexical signal into noise on top of a near-constant, which dropped lex from first to absent for the query "lex extraction"
  cannot_infer a learned embedding -> a better ranking; on one-line descriptions it measured worse, and the reason is the corpus rather than the model
  witness the discovery witness asserts the default metric is the one that measured better, so a future change that promotes the learned path has to beat it first
  known_gap the semantic path is measured on ten queries and that is a small set. It is enough to decide which metric leads and not enough to characterise either
  known_gap a capability is embedded from a one-line description. The atlas holds invariants, forbidden inferences and internal calculi for the same organ, and none of that reaches the vector
